<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">cargo</ansi>

Your company shares one pool of carrying capacity. Everything counts
toward it by its actual weight: what you carry and wear, what every
living companion with you carries and wears, and whatever sits in the
company's shared cargo.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How much you can carry</ansi>

Every member of the company carries a share:

  - 20 kg each, plus half a kilogram for every point of Strength
    (a companion who isn't out with you right now carries the 20 kg
    and their pack, without the Strength).
  - One pack each: a satchel adds 5 kg, a traveller's pack 10 kg, and
    a frame pack 15 kg. A second pack on the same member is only
    weight. Every new character starts with a satchel; give a pack to
    a companion to raise their share.
  - Horses add their own (<ansi fg="command">help mount</ansi>): a pack horse carries 40 kg,
    or 100 kg with a pack saddle.

A fallen companion, or one charmed away from you, carries nothing.
Recruiting raises your capacity; dismissing lowers it.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">When you're full</ansi>

At full capacity nobody can pick up, buy, or be handed anything more
until something is dropped, sold, or eaten. Walking is never blocked:
if a companion leaves and you're suddenly over, you can still walk,
only slowly. Moving things between your pack and the cargo, and giving
to your own companions, never changes the total, so they always work.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Being heavily loaded</ansi>

Past three quarters of capacity, journeys along a route take a little
longer and walking strains everyone a little more
(<ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>); past nine tenths, noticeably more; at or over full
capacity, a lot more. A lightly loaded company never goes faster than
its ordinary pace.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">cargo</ansi>
  Your load against capacity, split between you, your companions, and
  the cargo; the capacity split between the members and the horses;
  any travel or strain penalty in effect; and what's in the cargo.

  <ansi fg="command">cargo put [item]</ansi>
  Moves an item from your pack into the shared cargo.

  <ansi fg="command">cargo take [item]</ansi>
  Moves an item from the cargo back into your pack.

A partly used item keeps its uses in the cargo: put in a waterskin with
three drinks left and it comes back out with three. The company eats
and drinks from the cargo first (<ansi fg="command">help company meal</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help company inventory</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help mount</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help encumbrance</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>.
